    <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Foundation</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mongol Empire was founded in the early 13th century on the steppes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongolia">Mongolia</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, born Temüjin around 1162. Emerging from a fragmented world of rival Mongol and Turkic tribes, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatars</a>, Keraits, Merkits and Naimans, he unified the steppe through alliances, military skill and ruthless suppression of opponents, and was proclaimed Genghis Khan at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kurultai">kurultai</a> (assembly) in 1206. Tradition credits him with a body of law known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yasa">Yassa</a>, though its exact form is uncertain. The empire's rise was enabled by the steppe way of life, which produced highly mobile and disciplined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>, and by the divisions among its settled neighbours: the Western Xia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jurchen-jin-dynasty">Jurchen Jin dynasty</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> and the newly expanded but poorly integrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Control of pastures and trade routes reinforced both economic and military capacity. By breaking up old tribal loyalties, reorganising his followers into new decimal units and promoting commanders for loyalty and ability, Genghis Khan established centralised authority over a flexible tribal hierarchy, enabling rapid and sustained conquest across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> and into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#karakorum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karakorum.jpg" alt="Karakorum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#karakorum">Karakorum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erdene_Zuu_Monastery_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mongol society was shaped by its pastoral nomadic economy, organised around extended families, herds and seasonal migration. Horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#camel">camels</a>, sheep, goats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> provided food, transport and military advantage, while trade supplied grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a>, ceramics and metals that the steppe could not produce. Society was hierarchical, with the Borjigin royal clan and a warrior aristocracy at the top, common herders forming the backbone of the economy, and captives, including many skilled artisans, transferred from conquered lands. Conquered cities retained their administrative, craft and commercial functions, linking sedentary economies to the Mongol network, and the new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karakorum">Karakorum</a>, founded under Ögedei in the 1230s, drew merchants and craftsmen from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a> imposed taxation and tribute in grain, textiles and precious metals, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#census">censuses</a> across the empire. Long-distance trade, encouraged through partnerships with merchant associations (ortogh) and protected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pax-mongolica">Pax Mongolica</a>, enabled relatively safe passage along routes connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, creating unprecedented economic interconnection in Eurasia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Military</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#subutai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/subutai.jpg" alt="Subutai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#subutai">Subutai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Subudei.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">неизвестен/unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mongol military was highly organised, disciplined and mobile, its core composed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> drawn from the steppe tribes and augmented by units from conquered peoples. Armies were divided into decimal units (arban, zuun, mingghan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tumen">tumen</a>) for command and control, ensuring flexibility and scalability. Tactics included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#feigned-retreat">feigned retreats</a>, coordinated multi-pronged assaults and the extensive use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-engine">siege engines</a> operated by Chinese and Persian engineers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> (1219–1221) demonstrated mastery of logistics, reconnaissance and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#psychological-warfare">psychological warfare</a>, while generals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#subutai">Subutai</a> and Jebe defeated the Rus and Kipchaks at the Kalka River in 1223, and Subutai later led the western campaign that devastated the Rus principalities and defeated Polish and Hungarian armies at Legnica and Mohi in 1241. Hülegü's campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258. The army relied on promotion by merit, strict discipline and rapid communication through the yam relay system. Cavalry exploited speed, manoeuvrability and coordinated archery to overcome numerically superior opponents. Military power was the primary instrument of expansion, political consolidation and the enforcement of tribute across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ariq-boke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariq-boke.jpg" alt="Ariq Böke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ariq_B%C3%B6ke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Miskin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Government of the Mongol Empire was centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-khan">Great Khan</a>, elected by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kurultai">kurultai</a> of the royal family, but relied on extensive delegation to princes, subordinate khans and regional administrators. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yasa">Yassa</a>, traditionally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, provided a framework for military discipline and social order, and the adoption of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uyghurs">Uyghur</a> script gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a> their first written administration. Conquered peoples supplied bureaucrats such as the Khitan minister Yelü Chucai, engineers and tax collectors, while resident overseers known as darughachi supervised local rulers and revenue. Diplomatic correspondence and the yam relay network made government over vast distances possible. The Mongols generally tolerated diverse faiths, granting tax exemptions to clergy in return for prayers for the khan. After the succession war between Kublai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> (1260–1264), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> operated as largely independent states, though they continued to acknowledge a shared Chinggisid heritage. This combination of central legitimacy, delegated administration and pragmatic tolerance allowed the empire to hold together, for a time, across unprecedented geographic and ethnic diversity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#marco-polo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marco-polo.jpg" alt="Marco Polo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#marco-polo">Marco Polo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frontispice_%C3%A9dition_de_Nuremberg_1477_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mongol Empire profoundly reshaped Eurasian history, enabling unprecedented cross-continental interaction in trade, culture and technology. By securing the overland routes under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pax-mongolica">Pax Mongolica</a>, it allowed goods, ideas and people to travel between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, from merchants such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> to envoys such as the Franciscan William of Rubruck, and fostered exchanges in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>, medicine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cartography">cartography</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gunpowder">gunpowder</a> technology. The same networks may also have helped carry the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-death">Black Death</a> westwards in the 1340s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a> shaped successor states by combining steppe traditions with sedentary administration, notably in Yuan China and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> compiled a pioneering world history. Population movements, including the transfer of skilled artisans and administrators, accelerated cultural integration. Mongol campaigns redrew political boundaries, destroying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, the Jin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#song-dynasty">Song</a> dynasties and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, and subjecting the Rus principalities to more than two centuries of tributary rule. Mongol patronage of commerce and communication laid foundations for the wider networks of exchange of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#red-turban-rebellion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion.jpg" alt="Red Turban Rebellion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban Rebellion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Turban.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SY, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The unified Mongol Empire began to fragment in the mid-13th century, driven by overextension, succession disputes and the difficulty of governing vast territories. After the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongke">Great Khan Möngke</a> in 1259, the succession war between Kublai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> and conflicts between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> broke the empire into independent khanates pursuing separate policies. Kublai's death in 1294 removed the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-khan">Great Khan</a> widely recognised across the empire. Further succession struggles, rebellions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>, and economic strains, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inflation">inflation</a> of Yuan paper currency, famines and the ravages of plague, reduced the khanates' capacity to sustain armies and tribute networks. The Ilkhanate disintegrated after Abu Sa'id's death in 1335, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> was driven from China in 1368 by the Ming, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, a leader who had risen in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban Rebellion</a>. The Golden Horde fractured into rival khanates and lost its hold over Muscovy, symbolically ended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-stand-on-the-ugra-river">Great Stand on the Ugra River</a> in 1480. Although Mongol khans persisted in the steppe, the empire had long ceased to function as a single polity, its collapse revealing the limits of steppe-based rule over diverse and sedentary populations.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary"><em>The Mongol Empire stands as the steppe power that, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s unification of the tribes in 1206, joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> in a single network of trade and exchange under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pax-mongolica">Pax Mongolica</a>, and whose break-up into rival khanates showed the limits of steppe-based rule over vast settled populations.</em></p>

